     In order to analyze the "Great Coach" effect, we constructed a logistic regression model
                                  号


for fitting, and incorporated the Event-study method into the conventional Difference-in-Dif-
                                                             od
ferences (DID) approach to design a dynamic DID method capable of effectively estimating
                           众



the effect across multiple treatment groups. To mitigate the bias due to covariate differences,
                                             Hm

we included the Inverse Probability Weighting (IPW) method for estimating treatment effects,
              公




and applied the Double Robust Estimator (DR) framework to develop the DRD-CE model.
Performance analysis of this model showed an R-squared value of 0.756, indicating good fit,
and a "Great Coach" regression coefficient of 3.1084, highlighting the substantial impact of
                                          AT
             信




the "Great Coach" effect on specific countries and projects. Furthermore, we identified signif-
icant effects in countries such as the United States in Women's Sabre Fencing, Japan in
      微




Women's Gymnastics, Germany in Women's Sabre Fencing, and Field Hockey.
                                    M
                       ：




     Subsequently, we observed that changes in the number of smaller events within major
categories had a significant impact on the medal outcomes of some smaller countries. Ap-
proximately 104 nations are highly dependent on the variety of sports events to secure gold
                      号




medals. Based on these findings, we proposed to the National Olympic Committees the pro-
tection and expansion of their advantageous sports events.
         信




     Finally, we assessed and expanded the model's strengths and limitations, as well as ana-
        微




lyzed its sensitivity. By conducting error analysis between the total medal count derived from
the sum of gold, silver, and bronze medals and the total medal count directly input into the
model, we obtained an average error of 8.0783%. This result indicates that our model
demonstrates high sensitivity.

Keywords: Olympic Games; Tobit regression; Hurdle model; Great coach effect; Doubly ro-
bust estimator; Host country effect
