     The number of times a country participates in the Olympic Games not only reflects its
level of engagement but also implies its long-term athletic strength. To quantify this, ̅̅̅̅̅
                                                                                        𝑵𝑴𝒊 is
calculated by dividing the total number of Olympics a country has participated in by the total
number of Olympic Games since the first edition, thereby measuring the country’s sustained
involvement in the Olympics throughout history.

5.2 The Establishment of Model 1
     Inspired by the works of [3,4], we developed the Tobit-Mundlak- Hurdle Olympic
Medal Prediction Model (TMH-OMP) to provide accurate forecasts of Olympic medal




                                                                 型
counts. In the dataset, we observed a large number of countries that have yet to win any med-
als. To mitigate the impact of data from countries without medals on the predictions for coun-




                                                               模
tries with medals, we employed the Tobit regression model to address the truncation issue in
medal counts. Meanwhile, recognizing that a country's Olympic performance may be pro-




                                                             学
foundly influenced by constant, country-specific factors, we incorporated the Mundlak cor-
rection. By adding the time averages of country-specific variables, we capture these time-in-




                                                           数
variant effects. Finally, to predict the country that will win a medal for the first time, and the
probability of winning a medal. Hurdle models for countries are constructed that have histor-
                                                       ：

                                                      el s
ically won fewer than ten total medals.As shown in the figure 5.
